 - **Observational.** Higher total folate intake from foods and supplements is associated with 16% lower CRC risk (RR 0.84, 95% CI 0.80–0.90); the separate estimate for dietary folate from foods alone is 12% lower risk (RR 0.88, 95% CI 0.81–0.96). Dietary-folate analyses also reported inverse associations among alcohol consumers.<sup>[[27]](#ref27)[[29]](#ref29)[[30]](#ref30)</sup>
 - **Supplementation trials.** Folic acid has not reduced adenoma recurrence in RCTs and may increase risk of advanced adenomas and serrated polyps.<sup>[[28]](#ref28)</sup>
-- **B-PROOF follow-up.** Among 2,524 older trial participants, allocation to folic acid 400 μg plus B12 500 μg/day was associated with overall cancer HR 1.25 (95% CI 1.00–1.53) and CRC HR 1.77 (1.08–2.90). This secondary follow-up of a fracture trial cannot isolate an effect of folic acid alone or be directly extrapolated to routine pregnancy supplementation.<sup>[[31]](#ref31)</sup>
