 The 2024 Cochrane review found very uncertain evidence for zinc supplementation in pressure-ulcer healing. Its zinc-alone evidence came from two small older trials; evidence for combinations of protein, arginine, zinc, and antioxidants was also uncertain. A change in a collagen marker in a multinutrient trial cannot identify zinc's effect or establish fewer clinical wound failures.<sup>[[7]](#ref7)</sup>
 
-For incontinence-associated skin exposure in acute care, zinc oxide paste or cream can protect perineal skin from urine or stool when applied according to product instructions.<sup>[[27]](#ref27)</sup> An Unna boot is a leg-ulcer dressing and compression approach and is not a standard pelvic or intravaginal wound treatment.<sup>[[8]](#ref8)</sup>
+For incontinence-associated skin exposure in acute care, zinc oxide paste or cream can protect perineal skin from urine or stool when applied according to product instructions.<sup>[[27]](#ref27)</sup>
 
 For peristomal skin beneath an adhesive appliance, address fit and leakage first. WOCN guidance uses appropriately applied barrier powder, with optional alcohol-free barrier film, for moist skin injury. Zinc cream is not substituted beneath the wafer by default; products must be compatible with the appliance, and an ostomy nurse should be involved.<sup>[[9]](#ref9)</sup>
 
