--- a/docs/02-evaluation/imaging/ultrasound.mdx
+++ b/docs/02-evaluation/imaging/ultrasound.mdx
@@ -223,7 +223,7 @@
 
 A curved-array transducer (about 3.5 to 8 MHz) is placed on the perineum with the patient in dorsal lithotomy or supine. Three- and four-dimensional volumes are needed for levator assessment. Acquisitions are usually made at rest, on maximal Valsalva and on pelvic-floor contraction, with bladder filling adjusted to the question.
 
-One example protocol images the bladder at about 150 mL during urodynamic filling and again near capacity when vesicoureteral reflux is in question, with dynamic translabial images then obtained standing at rest and with Valsalva or cough, bladder-neck descent measured relative to the inferior pubic symphysis, and PVR measured at the end of the study. For prolapse imaging, at least 200 mL in the bladder with static images followed by rest and Valsalva views has been described. Excessive transducer pressure can distort anatomy or obscure prolapse. These are example acquisition parameters adapted to the clinical question, not validated universal thresholds.<sup>[[30]](#ref30)</sup>
