 ### Very Low Relative Function
 
-In a retrospective adult UPJO cohort with DRF ≤15%, 19 patients underwent pyeloplasty and 44 nephrectomy. Symptoms improved in both groups; mean DRF in the pyeloplasty group was 9.5% before surgery and 10% after surgery, without significant improvement. This supports selected organ preservation and is not a promise of functional recovery or a claim of treatment equivalence; allocation was nonrandomized and follow-up was short.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
+In a retrospective adult UPJO cohort with DRF ≤15%, 19 patients underwent pyeloplasty and 44 nephrectomy. Symptoms improved in both groups; mean DRF in the pyeloplasty group was 9.5% before surgery and 10% after surgery, without significant improvement.
 
 A low-functioning renal unit is therefore not automatically “nonfunctional,” and SRF &lt;15% is not a universal nephrectomy indication.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup> Conversely, reconstruction may not provide worthwhile benefit in every such kidney. Before nephrectomy, assess absolute or global and contralateral function; a contralateral percentage above 50% merely describes its relative share.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
 
